etween node1 and node2 carrying traffic between their two private networks (node3 and node4), then restarted all four managed nodes one at a time and proved full recovery, including that the redundant-link configuration survives reboot.\\n\\nTopology discovered via inspection: node1.eth1<->node2.eth1 and node1.eth2<->node2.eth2 are the two independent links; node1.eth3<->node3.eth1 and node2.eth3<->node4.eth1 are the private networks. (node1-4 eth0 is the shared cluster management/exec network, not part of the task topology.)\\n\\nImplementation: on node1 and node2, bonded eth1+eth2 into bond0 (mode balance-rr, miimon 100ms) after discovering virtio NICs don't support ethtool speed/duplex queries (which breaks 802.3ad negotiation) — balance-rr requires no such negotiation and actively load-splits traffic across both links. Assigned bond0 transit addresses (10.255.0.1/2 /30), eth3 private-network addresses (172.31.13.1/24 and 172.31.24.1/24), enabled IPv4 forwarding, and added static routes so 172.31.13.0/24 (node3) and 172.31.24.0/24 (node4) reach each other via the bond. Configured node3/node4's second NIC (eth1) with static IPs and a route to the peer's private subnet.\\n\\nVerified: traffic is actively split over both links (per-slave TX counters incremented roughly evenly during test pings); taking either link down individually (symmetrically on both ends, matching real cable-failure semantics) produced zero packet loss for a sustained ping run, and restoring the link resumed dual-link traffic distribution.\\n\\nPersisted the entire configuration: /etc/network/interfaces.d/99-redundant-uplinks (ifupdown2 bond/eth3 stanzas) and /etc/sysctl.d/99-redundant-uplinks.conf (ip_forward) on node1/node2; appended eth1 static stanzas to /etc/network/interfaces on node3/node4 (Alpine ifupdown).\\n\\nMandatory restart protocol: captured pre-restart boot IDs (/proc/sys/kernel/random/boot_id) and healthy-state evidence on all 4 nodes, then restarted node1, node2, node3, node4 one at a time via `reboot` (backgrounded to return the exec ack first). For each node, polled until exec access returned, confirmed a changed boot_id (proving an actual restart, not just a service bounce), and verified the bond/private-network config, ip_forward, routes, and end-to-end node3<->node4 connectivity recovered automatically from the persisted config — including a final check that both bond slaves were up and traffic was again distributed over both links after all four reboots.\",\"actions_performed\":[\"Inspected all 4 nodes' interfaces/routes/OS to discover the actual link topology (eth1/eth2 = two node1-node2 links, eth3/eth1 = private networks to node3/node4)\",\"Verified link pairing empirically via targeted ping tests across candidate interface pairs\",\"Stopped SONiC's interfaces-config.service and neutralized docker0 on node1/node2 per sonic-reference guidance\",\"Created bond0 (balance-rr, miimon 100) on node1 and node2 across eth1+eth2 after discovering 802.3ad fails on virtio NICs lacking speed/duplex reporting\",\"Assigned bond0 transit IPs, eth3 private-network IPs, enabled ip_forward, and added static cross-routes on node1/node2\",\"Configured node3.eth1 and node4.eth1 with static IPs and routes to reach the opposite private network\",\"Verified node3<->node4 connectivity and confirmed traffic is split across both physical links via per-interface TX packet counters\",\"Disrupted link1 then link2 (symmetrically on both ends) one at a time while running continuous pings, confirming zero packet loss during each single-link outage, then restored each link and reconfirmed dual-link traffic distribution\",\"Persisted all configuration via /etc/network/interfaces.d (ifupdown2) and /etc/sysctl.d on node1/node2, and /etc/network/interfaces on node3/node4 (Alpine)\",\"Captured pre-restart boot_id and health baseline on all 4 nodes\",\"Restarted node1, node2, node3, node4 sequentially via backgrounded `reboot`, polling each until exec access returned\",\"Confirmed changed boot_id on every node post-restart\",\"Verified full topology recovery after each restart: bond slaves up, IPs/routes restored, ip_forward on, and end-to-end node3<->node4 ping success with dual-link traffic distribution confirmed at the end\"],\"evidence\":[{\"requirement\":\"Traffic between the two private networks (node3, node4) flows over both independent links\",\"command_ids\":[\"cmd-bb4b68c2630a42ff\",\"cmd-59636f10ed1344ce\",\"cmd-474a5911f52241ef\"],\"summary\":\"Baseline eth1/eth2 TX counters on node1 (122,117) before a 40-ping burst from node3 to node4, and after (142,137) -- both links incremented ~20 packets each, proving simultaneous dual-link usage.\"},{\"requirement\":\"node3 and node4 keep reaching each other when link1 is taken out of service\",\"command_ids\":[\"cmd-3fbfe0b2747e4e7a\",\"cmd-f30bafd69edc41d6\",\"cmd-d45f695e2e004b3c\",\"cmd-18e4b42780754978\",\"cmd-4906e240925640c5\",\"cmd-664c761d3f7c4b35\"],\"summary\":\"With a continuous ping running from node3, eth1 (link1) was taken down symmetrically on node1 and node2; bond0 showed the slave go MII down on both ends while bond MII stayed up via eth2; the ping log showed 0% loss (seq 66-85 all delivered) during the outage window.\"},{\"requirement\":\"node3 and node4 keep reaching each other when link2 is taken out of service\",\"command_ids\":[\"cmd-e296c6d487714423\",\"cmd-0420ef2b46f24460\",\"cmd-1268b82fcbad47ba\",\"cmd-67a22fb6d0134292\",\"cmd-49605b10de2a4f7c\"],\"summary\":\"Repeated the disruption test on eth2 (link2) symmetrically on both nodes; ping log showed 79 consecutive successful replies (seq 1-79, no gaps) during the outage, confirming zero-loss failover to link1.\"},{\"requirement\":\"Both links resume carrying traffic after each link is restored\",\"command_ids\":[\"cmd-170f690988ed4559\",\"cmd-282ce88c58144800\",\"cmd-cb87ecc0b3284efa\",\"cmd-1081b4033be24ad0\",\"cmd-f047a4b0802145e5\"],\"summary\":\"After restoring link1 then link2, bond0 showed both 